# Types a new timer's label using the on-screen touch keyboard
# (multitap_keyboard/multitap_keyboard.c), not the physical-button
# multitap flow. Sets a duration via TOUCH first (touch_dial_set_via_
# position.seq's own mechanism - only incidental here), which by default
# opens the label keyboard automatically once committed: main.c's
# s_keyboard_on_new_timer defaults to TRUE (new_flow_open_label_cb), unlike
# the OTHER touch_dial_*.seq sequences, which deliberately push
# KeyboardOnNewTimer=0 to SKIP this screen - this sequence is the one that
# actually exercises it, so it must NOT set that key.
#
# The keyboard is a 3x4 touch grid (multitap_keyboard.c's prv_grid_metrics,
# for this app's 200x228 screen: origin (1, 46), cell size 66x45 - derived
# from TEXT_AREA_H=44 + GRID_VPAD=2 above the grid, GRID_VPAD=2 below, and
# the 200-wide screen split into 3 equal columns). Keys 0-8 are letter keys
# (PAGE_KEY's ".,?!"/"abc"/"def"/"ghi"/"jkl"/"mno"/"pqrs"/"tuv"/"wxyz", row-
# major); each repeated TAP on the SAME key cycles its letters (m -> n ->
# o), while a tap on a DIFFERENT key commits whatever was pending first
# (multitap_keyboard.c's prv_press_char_key) - no waiting between key
# presses is needed for that. Only the FINAL pending letter needs an
# explicit commit, which the physical SELECT button's submit
# (multitap_keyboard_window.c's prv_select_click -> multitap_keyboard_
# submit) does on its own - there is no on-screen touch "OK"/submit key by
# design (see multitap_keyboard.h's own header comment).
#
# Each TOUCH here uses a SHORT hold (0.1s), not the ~0.6s used against the
# touch DIAL elsewhere in this suite - this widget's own tap-vs-hold
# threshold (multitap_keyboard_window.c's HOLD_MS=350) is the opposite
# requirement: a hold here inserts that key's keypad DIGIT
# (multitap_keyboard_handle_hold) instead of cycling its letter, which
# would silently corrupt the typed label. 0.1s is comfortably under 350ms.
#
# Container-only: run via
#   tests/functional/docker/run_container.sh \
#     tests/functional/sequences/walkthroughs/touch_keyboard_label.seq --touch
# See functional_framework/README.md's TOUCH row and
# tests/functional/docker/README.md's TOUCH section for why.
IMPORT ../common/wipe_and_prep.seq

# Stop RunOnCreate/AutoReturnStart from racing this sequence's own
# screenshots (same as create_and_start_timer.seq) - deliberately NOT
# KeyboardOnNewTimer=0, see the header comment above.
APPMSG --int 10015=0 10003=0
APPMSG --int 10033=0 --string 10031="12:00"
SCREENSHOT list_empty

TAP select
APPMSG --int 10033=0 --string 10031="12:00"
SCREENSHOT duration_dial_default

# Same "15 minutes" dial point as touch_dial_set_via_position.seq (see its
# own comment for the derivation) - the duration value itself is
# incidental here; this test's real subject is the keyboard that opens
# next.
#
# Unlike touch_dial_set_via_position.seq (which sets KeyboardOnNewTimer=0
# and returns straight to the main list after the dial's own ~0.5s settle
# - see its own comment), this sequence deliberately lets the keyboard
# open, which is an ADDITIONAL window push on top of the dial's commit/
# close animation.
#
# Uses the split TOUCHDOWN/SLEEP/TOUCHUP form here, NOT the atomic TOUCH
# helper (contrast the comment above run_touch() in
# functional_framework/lib/steps.sh) - live-verified the atomic form
# (mousedown+sleep+mouseup chained inside a single xdotool invocation) was
# genuinely flaky landing this specific dial-commit-then-keyboard-open
# transition: reproduced directly across repeated containerized runs,
# still showing the duration dial instead of the keyboard regardless of
# how long a SLEEP followed it (tried up to 1.5s with no improvement) -
# not purely a post-touch settle problem. Two changes together fixed it,
# confirmed clean across 6 consecutive containerized runs (0/6 flaky,
# vs. roughly 1-in-4 before):
# 1. Splitting into two separate xdotool invocations (TOUCHDOWN, a real
#    host-level SLEEP, then a separate TOUCHUP) instead of one chained
#    xdotool call - matching touch_dial_set_via_position.seq's own
#    pattern (the one other touch sequence in this suite using a real
#    hold, which was never flaky) - this alone roughly halved the
#    failure rate but didn't eliminate it (still ~1-in-4).
# 2. Lengthening the hold from 0.6s (this app's touchLiftMinDurationMs
#    tap-vs-hold threshold is 100ms, so 0.6s was already well clear of
#    that on paper) to 1.2s - eliminated the remainder. Exact root cause
#    unconfirmed (plausibly xdotool/X11/qemu event-timing jitter under
#    container load, not a gesture-recognition threshold issue per se,
#    since 0.6s already cleared the app's own threshold with margin) -
#    if this ever regresses, re-verify with several repeated runs (a
#    single run passing or failing proves nothing either way here).
TOUCHDOWN 170 135
SLEEP 1.2
TOUCHUP
SLEEP 1
APPMSG --int 10033=0 --string 10031="12:00"
SCREENSHOT keyboard_open

# "O" is the 3rd letter of key5 ("mno", cell centre (166, 113): row 1,
# col 2 -> x = 1 + 2*66 + 33 = 166, y = 46 + 1*45 + 22 = 113) - 3 quick
# taps cycle m -> n -> o.
TOUCH 166 113 0.1
TOUCH 166 113 0.1
TOUCH 166 113 0.1

# "K" is the 2nd letter of key4 ("jkl", cell centre (100, 113): row 1,
# col 1 -> x = 1 + 1*66 + 33 = 100, same y). Switching keys here commits
# the pending "o" first (prv_press_char_key), then 2 quick taps cycle
# j -> k.
TOUCH 100 113 0.1
TOUCH 100 113 0.1
APPMSG --int 10033=0 --string 10031="12:00"
SCREENSHOT keyboard_typed

# SELECT commits the pending "k" and submits the whole label - see
# multitap_keyboard_window.c's prv_select_click / multitap_keyboard_submit.
TAP select
SLEEP 0.5
APPMSG --int 10033=0 --string 10031="12:00"
SCREENSHOT timer_created
